                          "Half the nurses would come."
                        ],
                        "answer": 0
                      },
                      {
                        "question": "\"I'd rather not.\" means…",
                        "options": [
                          "I'd rather not have a big wedding.",
                          "I'd rather not cook."
                        ],
                        "answer": 0
                      },
                      {
                        "question": "\"Have you and Daniel?\" means…",
                        "options": [
                          "Have you and Daniel chosen a date?",
                          "Have you and Daniel moved?"
                        ],
                        "answer": 0
                      }
                    ]
                  }
                },
                {
                  "phase": "grammar",
                  "type": "grammar_rule",
                  "content": {
                    "title": "Ellipsis and substitution",
                    "explanation": "En la conversación los nativos evitan repetir lo que ya está claro:\n• so / not sustituyen una frase entera: I hope so. I think so. I'd rather not. I hope not.\n• El auxiliar solo: Would you like to cook? — I would. You'll be my bridesmaid? — Of course I will.\n• to sin verbo: If you'd like to. I'd have loved to. if you want us to.\n• So / Neither + auxiliar + sujeto: So would half the nurses. Neither have I.\n• Preguntas cortas: Have you and Daniel? (chosen a date)\n\nResponder con la frase completa («Yes, I would like to cook») suena muy poco natural.",
                    "table": {
                      "headers": [
                        "Recurso",
                        "Ejemplo",
                        "Frase completa"
                      ],
                      "rows": [
                        [
                          "so / not",
                          "I hoped so. / I'd rather not.",
                          "I hoped he'd propose. / I'd rather not have a big wedding."
                        ],
                        [
                          "auxiliar solo",
                          "I would. / Of course I will.",
                          "I would like to cook. / I will be your bridesmaid."
                        ],
                        [
                          "to sin verbo",
                          "If you'd like to. / I'd have loved to.",
                          "If you'd like to cook. / I'd have loved to see that."
                        ],
                        [
                          "So / Neither + auxiliar + sujeto",
                          "So would half the nurses.",
                          "Half the nurses would also have loved to see it."
                        ],
                        [
                          "pregunta corta",
                          "Have you and Daniel?",
                          "Have you and Daniel chosen a date?"
                        ]
                      ]
                    },
                    "examples": [
                      "I hoped so.",
                      "So would half the nurses.",
                      "I think so. My mum wants five hundred guests. I'd rather not.",
                      "Of course I will."
                    ]
                  }
                },
                {
                  "phase": "grammar",
                  "type": "grammar_rule",
                  "content": {
                    "title": "Vague language: sort of, or so, -ish, or something",
                    "explanation": "En la conversación los nativos no siempre son exactos, y sonar demasiado preciso puede resultar frío. El lenguaje aproximativo suaviza y aproxima:\n\n• Cantidades: about / around / roughly 500, 500 or so, 30-odd people (unos treinta y pico), dozens of, loads of.\n• Horas y edades con -ish: Let's meet at sevenish. He's fortyish.\n• Adjetivos con -ish o kind of / sort of: It's greenish. I'm kind of nervous. It's sort of a party, sort of a dinner.\n• Listas abiertas: and stuff, and things like that, or something (like that), and so on: We need flowers, music and stuff. Let's have tapas or something.\n• Cosas cuyo nombre no recordamos: the thingy, whatsit: Pass me the thingy for the cake.\n\nOjo: en un correo formal o un informe, este registro es demasiado informal; usa approximately o around.",
                    "table": {
                      "headers": [
                        "Para…",
                        "Ejemplo"
                      ],
                      "rows": [
                        [
                          "Cantidades",
                          "There'll be five hundred guests or so."
                        ],
                        [
                          "Horas / edades",
                          "Come at eightish. He's thirty-odd."
                        ],
                        [
                          "Atenuar",
                          "I'm kind of nervous about the speech."
                        ],
                        [
                          "Listas abiertas",
                          "We need music, flowers and stuff."
                        ],
                        [
                          "Opciones abiertas",
                          "Let's get pizza or something."
                        ]
                      ]
                    },
                    "examples": [
                      "My mum wants five hundred guests or so.",
                      "Let's meet at the restaurant at eightish.",
                      "It was kind of romantic, kind of embarrassing.",
                      "We'll need a band, flowers and stuff like that."
                    ]
                  }
                },
